.TH WHOSENT 1 "2026-10-10" "whosent manual" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whosent \- see who sent an agent, and what a delegation chain still lets it do
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whosent
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\fR]
.br
.B whosent check
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-strict\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\fR]
.br
.B whosent ask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIREQUEST OPTIONS\fR] [\fIFILE\fR | \fB\-\fR]
.br
.B whosent demo
[\fB\-\-fixed\fR] [\fB\-\-list\fR] [\fB\-\-emit\fR \fIDIR\fR [\fB\-\-force\fR]] [\fISCENARIO\fR]
.br
.B whosent completion
\fISHELL\fR
.br
.B whosent version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whosent
opens a Mandatum delegation chain in a terminal UI: a series of signed
assertions running from an authenticated human sponsor down to the agent that
is acting. It shows the human the chain is rooted in, how authority narrows at
every hop, and where trust breaks.
.PP
Every verification rule of the Mandatum specification, V1 to V9, is evaluated
for every link. Unlike a verifier, which stops at the first failure, whosent
reports all of them, and draws everything below the first broken link as
unreached: no authority reaches it.
.PP
A rule that needs an input you did not give is reported as
.IR "not checked" ,
never as passing. Signatures need keys (\fB\-\-trust\fR or \fB\-\-jwks\fR),
revocation needs a published set (\fB\-\-revocations\fR), and the audience rule
needs the resource server's identifier (\fB\-\-audience\fR). A chain is only
reported
.B VALID
when every rule was checked for every link and held.
.PP
On every run, whosent also hands the same chain and the same answers to the
Mandatum v0.2.0 reference verifier it embeds. If the two disagree, the
reference verdict wins, the
disagreement is shown as an internal inconsistency, and the exit status is 70.
.SH INPUT
The chain is read from \fIFILE\fR, or from standard input when \fIFILE\fR is
\fB\-\fR or standard input is a pipe. The format is recognised by its shape:
.TP
.B JSON array of strings
The wire form: compact JWS serializations, sponsor first.
.TP
.B MCP tools/call request
A JSON-RPC request whose \fBparams._meta\fR carries the chain under
\fBio.github.kanywst.mandatum/chain\fR, read exactly as mandatum's MCP
middleware reads it. The tool name and arguments are captured for \fBask\fR.
Bare params, a bare \fB_meta\fR, and \fB{"chain": [...]}\fR are accepted too.
.TP
.B One serialization per line
Blank lines and lines starting with \fB#\fR are skipped.
.PP
Bytes inside a serialization are never altered, since a link commits to its
parent by a digest over the parent's exact bytes. Input is limited to 1 MiB and
64 links.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B check
Evaluate every rule for every link and print a report: the verdict, a rule grid
per link, every finding, and how authority narrows at each hop. With
\fB\-\-json\fR the report is printed as JSON (schema
.BR whosent.report/v1 )
and nothing else reaches standard output.
.TP
.B ask
Check a request against the chain with mandatum's own matcher, at every link,
so you can see where authority for it ends, or where it appears from nowhere.
The answer is labelled
.B AUTHORITATIVE
(the chain verified with every input supplied),
.B CONDITIONAL
(nothing failed, but some rules were not checked) or
.B HYPOTHETICAL
(the chain does not verify, so it permits nothing; this is what the leaf
claims). With \fB\-\-json\fR the answer is printed as schema
.BR whosent.ask/v1 .
.TP
.B demo
Mint example chains with throwaway keys and open them in the interactive view.
With no \fISCENARIO\fR it tours them all. Each scenario brings its own keys,
revocation set and audience, so the input options do not apply. With
\fB\-\-emit\fR \fIDIR\fR it writes one scenario's files instead:
.IR chain.json ,
.IR jwks/ ,
.IR trust.json ,
.IR revocation.json ,
.I revoked\-jtis.txt
and a
.I manifest.json
holding the exact
.B whosent check
command and what it must report. Files are written 0600 through a temporary
file and a rename, so a planted symbolic link is replaced rather than written
through; a directory that is not empty is refused without \fB\-\-force\fR.
Scenarios: healthy, widened, revoked, expired, spliced, custody, audience,
retarget, forged, multifault, unchecked.
.TP
.B completion
Print a completion script for \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, \fBfish\fR or
\fBpowershell\fR.
.TP
.B version
Print whosent's version and the version of the Mandatum reference verifier
it embeds.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-trust " FILE"
A trust file mapping issuers to their key documents:
.B {"issuers": {"ISSUER": {"jwks_file": "PATH"} | {"jwks_url": "https://..."}}}.
A relative \fBjwks_file\fR is resolved against the trust file's directory.
.TP
.BI \-\-jwks " ISSUER=PATH|ISSUER=https://URL"
One issuer's keys. Repeatable. Keys are read the way mandatum's JWKS resolver
reads them: Ed25519 only, and a document it would refuse is refused here.
URLs must be https, including after any redirect; a fetch is capped at 1 MiB
and 5 seconds. Issuers come only from these options, never from the chain, so
a chain cannot make whosent fetch anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-revocations " FILE"
A published compact revocation set (specification section 7.1).
.TP
.BI \-\-revoked\-jtis " FILE"
An exact list of revoked jtis, one per line, used to resolve a hit in the
revocation set's filter. Without it, a hit denies, as the specification says.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-age " DURATION"
The oldest revocation set accepted; \fB0\fR accepts any age. Default 24h. A
stale set makes revocation unknown, which denies.
.TP
.BI \-\-audience " AUDIENCE"
The resource server the chain is presented to; checks rule V9. An empty value
is an error.
.TP
.BI \-\-at " TIME"
Evaluate at this time instead of now: RFC 3339, unix seconds, \fBnow\fR, or a
relative offset such as \fB+25m\fR or \fB\-1h\fR. The revocation set's age is
measured against this time too.
.TP
.BI \-\-skew " DURATION"
Clock skew tolerance, at most 5m. Default 30s.
.TP
.B \-\-reverse
The chain was handed over leaf first; reverse it before checking. Without this,
a chain that looks reversed is reported, never silently reordered.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-motion
No animation in the interactive view.
.TP
.B \-\-ascii
ASCII glyphs only. Also set by \fBWHOSENT_ASCII=1\fR.
.SH CHECK OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the report as JSON (schema \fBwhosent.report/v1\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Treat INCOMPLETE as a failure (exit 1).
.SH ASK OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-resource\-type " TYPE"
The resource's type. Default \fBmcp_tool\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-resource\-id " ID"
The resource, such as a tool name. Defaults to the tool a tools/call request
names.
.TP
.BI \-\-action " NAME"
The action attempted. Default \fBinvoke\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-tag " TAG"
A tag the deployment attaches to the resource. Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-attr " KEY=VALUE"
An attribute conditions are evaluated against. \fIVALUE\fR is read as JSON when
it parses, so \fBargs.n=3\fR is a number. Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-\-attr\-from\-args
Map a tools/call request's arguments to \fBargs.\fR\fIname\fR attributes. This
mapping is whosent's assumption, not something mandatum's enforcement point
does, and a warning says so.
.TP
.BI \-\-request " FILE"
Read the request from a JSON file instead.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON (schema \fBwhosent.ask/v1\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Treat a CONDITIONAL answer as not permitted.
.SH RULES
.TP
.B V1
Structure: the link parses, is well formed, commits to its parent's digest, and
its depth follows the parent's.
.TP
.B V2
Custody: each link is issued by the subject of the link above it.
.TP
.B V3
Signature: the issuer signed these exact bytes, with a key you supplied.
.TP
.B V4
Time: not issued in the future and not expired, within the skew.
.TP
.B V5
Attenuation: authority only narrows. Capabilities, expiry, delegation depth,
sequence constraints and audience are no wider than the parent's.
.TP
.B V6
Sponsor: every link names the same human, authenticated the same way.
.TP
.B V7
Depth: the chain is no deeper than the sponsor allowed.
.TP
.B V8
Revocation: no link is revoked. Revoking a link revokes everything below it.
.TP
.B V9
Audience: the leaf is addressed to this resource server.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B check
exits:
.TP
.B 0
VALID: every rule was checked for every link, and holds.
.TP
.B 1
INVALID: a rule failed, or could not be evaluated (an enforcement point denies).
.TP
.B 2
INCOMPLETE: nothing failed, but something was not checked. 1 with
\fB\-\-strict\fR.
.TP
.B 3
The command line or an input file is wrong.
.TP
.B 70
whosent and mandatum disagree about the chain. This is a bug in whosent;
please report it.
.PP
.B ask
exits 0 when the request is permitted by a VALID chain, 1 when it is not
(including any request against an INVALID chain), 2 when it is permitted
conditionally on rules that were not checked (1 with \fB\-\-strict\fR), and 3
and 70 as above. The exit status is unchanged by \fB\-\-json\fR.
.SH KEY BINDINGS
.TP
.B \(<-/h, \(->/l
Select a link (an arrow on the rail).
.TP
.B \(ua/k, \(da/j
Select a row of authority.
.TP
.BR J ", " K ", " pgup ", " pgdn
Scroll the link panel.
.TP
.B tab
Switch panel, when they do not fit side by side.
.TP
.B r
The raw assertion: header, claims and digests. A parent digest takes the colour
of the link it matches, so a spliced link is the one whose colour matches
nothing.
.TP
.B space
Replay authority flowing down the chain.
.TP
.B t
Time mode: a timeline of every link, and \(<- \(-> move the clock. The engine is
re-run at each step.
.TP
.BR [ ", " ] ", " shift+\(<- ", " shift+\(->
Move the clock in big steps.
.TP
.B 0
Back to the time the chain was opened at.
.TP
.B x
Simulate revoking the selected link; again to undo. Nothing is revoked.
.TP
.B X
Clear every simulated revocation.
.TP
.B a
Ask whether the chain would permit a request.
.TP
.BR n ", " p
Next or previous scenario, in the demo tour.
.TP
.B ?
Show the full key binding help.
.TP
.BR q ", " esc
Quit, or go back.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to anything, draw without colour. Every state also has its own glyph,
so nothing is lost.
.TP
.B WHOSENT_ASCII
When set to 1, use ASCII glyphs only, as \fB\-\-ascii\fR does.
.SH SECURITY
Every string taken from a chain, a key document or a library error is escaped
before it reaches the terminal: control characters, escape sequences and bidi
overrides are shown as visible escapes, so a chain cannot rewrite the screen,
write the clipboard or disguise a name. \fB\-\-json\fR output is escaped the
same way.
.PP
whosent touches the network only to fetch key documents from the https URLs
you configure.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Take the tour:
.B whosent demo
.TP
Write a scenario out and check it the way a script would:
.B whosent demo \-\-emit fixture widened && cd fixture && whosent check chain.json \-\-trust trust.json \-\-revocations revocation.json \-\-audience https://mcp.example.org \-\-at 2026\-09\-12T08:01:00Z
.TP
Open a chain against your own keys:
.B whosent chain.json \-\-trust trust.json \-\-revocations revocation.json \-\-audience https://mcp.example.org
.TP
Gate CI on a chain that fully verifies:
.B whosent check chain.json \-\-trust trust.json \-\-revocations revocation.json \-\-audience https://mcp.example.org
.TP
Ask whether the leaf may fetch a page:
.B whosent ask chain.json \-\-resource\-id web.fetch \-\-tag external\-content \-\-attr args.host=docs.example.org
.TP
Read the chain straight out of an MCP request:
.B whosent ask call.json \-\-attr\-from\-args
